summary:
The draft is accurate. I found no critical or major errors, and nothing omitted or invented. Compared with the Latin, the numbers, names, ablative absolutes, AcI constructions and the corrupt or unclear passages are handled soundly, and the doubts section flags the real cruxes (ch. 17 'monitus', ch. 21 'inter regales delicias', ch. 24 'mulier consentiret', ch. 25 zeugma, ch. 39 'satrapas quidem et', ch. 41 'peditesque sequentes'). I read all of Valerius Book I against the notes, using the Kübler 1888 OCR text on archive.org. Every claim I checked against Valerius holds up:
- the Hephaestion, nine-competitor and Attalus details;
- Lysias' jibe and the 'labitur' detail;
- 'ecquid mulier consentiret' / 'neque id ... provenisset' and the Ambrosianus 'idque';
- the 70,604 / 70,654 figures and the 400-talent and 100-pound figures;
- the Hystaspes and Spinther names;
- Scamander and the Cassander emendations;
- the Ambrosianus variants (iudex/vindex, vel stultissimos dixerim, the marginal 'veste dignum eum duxerunt');
- 'cum tua aetatula'.
Dowden's translation also confirms that the Greek has the Isthmian games, Kleitomachos and Ismenias. The Kübler page references in the notes also match. The six items above are all minor, mostly referent or lexical precision. I did not check the Greek recension α claims in the notes against Kroll, so I cannot confirm those. I could not check Zacher's page image or apparatus, so Zacher's 'tuae' (ch. 36) and the manuscript sigla in the doubts remain unverified. I did not flag the ch. 36 'tuae' reading, because the draft documents it openly."
